  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>The set of numbers used to describe the position and energy of the electron in an atom are called quantum numbers. There are four quantum numbers, namely, principal, azimuthal, magnetic and spin quantum numbers.</p><p>Electronic configuration of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K</mi></math> is:</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K</mi><mo>=</mo><mo> </mo><mi>Atomic</mi><mo> </mo><mi>number</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>19</mn><mspace linebreak=\"newline\"></mspace><mn>1</mn><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mn>2</mn><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mn>2</mn><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mn>6</mn></msup><mn>3</mn><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mn>3</mn><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mn>6</mn></msup><mn>4</mn><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></math></p><p>The outermost electron is in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>4</mn><msup><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></math></p><p>∴ <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mo> </mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mo> </mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mo> </mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></math></p><p>Hence, the answer is option B.</p>",
    "image": null
  },
